  {
    "episode_id": 90437,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90437.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 6,
    "turn_text": "Well, again, thank you. That's just a salute. Postal Service. Postal Service is in the Constitution, and Congress' relationship with it is in the Constitution. This is what you would have said some years ago, all American is apple pie, motherhood, baseball, the Postal Service. Postal Service is the most popular agency of government, over 90 percent favorable of it. The President has shredded the Constitution, and now he wants to cut the sinews that have held our country together. When we were colonies, we didn't have a Postal Service. When we did, we became a united country. And so they're going right after that. It is interesting to see the response that members and Democrats and Republicans, House and Senate, all over the country are receiving because the Postal Service means something to the American people. So what I have called as members yesterday, put out the letter for members to come back This week we will be standing announced on our conference call. We will be voting on Saturday. The legislation will be complete tomorrow night and it's Congressional, that is, you know, alleged counsel, CBO, all that. But the terms of it address the concerns that we have and include $25 billion for the Postal Service. Now, it's interesting to note, I think, that $25 billion is a figure that was requested by the Board of Governors of the Postal Service. Democrats and Republicans, 100% appointed by Donald Trump. They asked for the $25 billion, actually even more. We're having someone another bill on our infrastructure bill, but the 25 that we need to have now. So that's the provenance of that number. And again, it has a direct relationship to the elections. People say and I agree, the Postal Service is election central at this time.",
