    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90437.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 36,
    "turn_text": "Well, let's just say anyone with children or grandchildren, in my case or in school, understands the angst that families have across the country. And one of the biggest obstacles to agreement for us is funding for education. We have a difference as to the amount, but also how the money is spent. In addition to that, unless you support state and local government, which is the other big obstacle to agreement, that's where over 90% of public education funding comes from state and local government. So there are two areas of big ideological disagreement. They say, why should one state help pay for the needs of another state? Well, welcome to the United States of America. Is Trevor here of Katrina, or Aik, or Doppler, Missouri, or North Carolina Storms, or anything like that? Yes, that's what we do. And B, The fact is that they're saying the president wants all the schools to open up actually. So the bulk of the money that they want to spend is for actual opening up children there in person. And say about 75% at least of the largest school districts in the country are going to be virtual or hybrid. So again, it's an ideological thing, so we're saying that you may not know this to them, but it takes almost the same amount of money to open actually, virtually, or in a hybrid way. And so we need more money, but don't take our word for it. This is the request of the American Association of school superintendents. Any of those organizations are saying we need more money than they want to put up, A, and we need it spent in a way that recognizes so much, but will be virtual. And at the same time, we need the state and local money, which is the largest, over 90% as I say, support for schools",
